Ratchet Quest VR: SCENERY AND WATER, every player's game draws its own water, seas, lava and effects; and SHOW COPIES,
what the host's enemies shoot and blow up into, and the orders level events give to scenery, shown in a member's game.

RAC1 draws each animated surface (water, seas, lava, fire and smoke, reflections) through a callback
its owner's update registers every tick, and the particles of a level's emitters come from theirs
(OpenRAC's docs/engine/systems/WORLD_ANIMATION.md). Multiplayer's member drove every placed moby
that runs an update, so those updates never ran there: a member saw no water on Novalis, no seas,
no lava, while the host's game drew all of it. This puts those owners in local_class (world.hpp
scenery_class, read class by class from rac1-decomp's updates): every game runs its own, a host
with this patch sends none of them, and a member skips a host's poses for them, so a host without
it is no different.

Water whose height the level's switches and floods move (world.hpp water_follow_class: Rilgar's,
Hoven's, Pokitaru's, Umbris's plates) is no longer driven either: a member runs its update, which
draws it, and puts it at the host's pose as before; when that height changes, the new
Game::water_follows calls the level's own code that puts the water patches and their collision
at the moby's height (game_adapter.cpp kWaterDiscs, both discs).

Show copies (world_show.cpp; world.hpp's "show copies" block says how): what the host's enemies
shoot, the blasts they go up in and what flies off them are mobys their updates make, not
creatures, so a member saw an enemy aim and then only its own health drop. A host with this patch
tells a member with it, on the same disc, of each one (the moby and its pvars), behind the entries
of a WORLD that has room; the member makes its own copy, which flies and bursts there while the
host's game alone hits, as before. Orders: scenery a level event gives an order to (its command
byte; world.hpp order_class, Hoven's reservoir) follows the host's, the member writing the order
that brings its own there. Only between two games of this build: the member says so after its
HELLO's digest (`'Q' 'V' version disc layout`), which a plain host does not read, and only such a
member's WORLD carries the tail, after what decode_world reads. Multiplayer's version and party
digest are unchanged, and nothing changes between this build and any other game.

Upkeep: the patch touches world.hpp (its QUEST-VR blocks, one line of local_class and World's
show members), world.cpp, world_gate.cpp, world_handle.cpp, world_host.cpp and world_member.cpp
(marked places), coop.hpp (virtuals at the end of Game), game_adapter (functions at the end, and
the show region beside progress_disc, apart from what patch 0002 changes), CMakeLists.txt
(world_show.cpp) and the new world_show.cpp. If upstream grows its own list of each game's
scenery, drop scenery_class and keep only what it misses; if upstream renames local_class, put
scenery_class in its replacement. A class added to either list, or an order_class, needs only
world.hpp. Its tests are tests/play_world_test.cpp scenery_and_water_are_each_games, the show
copies' tests and a_members_reservoir_drains_as_the_hosts (FakeGame in tests/test_support.hpp).
docs/MULTIPLAYER.md lists what each patch is for and what still shows only in the host's game.

